people are starting to panic and 2020 is  not looking like a great year for  developers the Dow was down over 2,000  points today and has been collapsing for  the last few weeks VCS are warning  founders to preserve cash all the big  tech conferences have been canceled  governments are talking about helicopter  cash and 0% interest rates and things  are deteriorating very quickly out there  in today's video we'll look at why and  more importantly what does it mean for  software developers in the future  everything we see in the headlines is  about coronavirus but we can't blame  everything on this virus the global  economy and the tech industry in  particular has been in a nearly non-stop  bull market for the last 10 years some  might even call it a bubble if we go  back to the early 2010's many of the big  public companies that we know today were  just starting to raise money from  private VCS  the successful startups always had  access to a huge amount of cash and were  able to focus on growth while operating  deep in the red or in other words not  make a profit for a really long time or  in most cases never make a profit around  2016 a lot of these VC funded startups  started to go public one of my favorite  examples is Shopify an IPO in 2015 at  $28 in 2020 it's traded well above $500  per share now the company's revenue has  grown significantly but so of expenses  and it's still not profitable today but  its market value has increased 20 times  on speculation and optimism and there  are many other tech companies in a  similar situation they continue to burn  through cash and are a long way from  profitability which is how you generate  new cash on your own without external  financing now I'm sure Shopify will turn  out just fine but there are plenty of  smaller companies out there with shorter  runways that may not be able to survive  a severe cash crunch Sequoia Capital one  of the most well-known VCS in Silicon  Valley released this black swan memo to  its founders I recommend reading the  whole thing on your own but basically it  boils down to a warning that companies  may not be able to burn through cash  like they have in the past and what's  one of the first things companies do  when they start to run out of cash well  they start laying people off employees  are very valuable but they're also very  expensive and at the end of the day cash  is king  when you run out of money the game is  over now at this point it's impossible  to predict if the stock market will  continue to fall but layoffs always lag  behind the activity in the stock market  during the last financial crisis the  jobless rate didn't start to rise until  well after the stock market had bottomed  out on top of that we've already seen  cost-cutting and downsizing among some  of the big tech unicorns so what does  all this mean for professional software  developers if you have a job today  great it's probably a good idea to  hold on to that job until you have  something better lined out if the  companies that have the best developer  jobs are cost-cutting it means there  will be fewer developer jobs going  around and it may drive down the value  of compensation and I would expect that  to have the biggest impact on junior  developer roles because we have a lot  more talent entering the industry  through things like coding boot camps  not to mention these coding boot camps  have been receiving a lot of criticism  lately primarily because of already poor  job placement rates and that will only  get worse in a bad economy it really  just comes down to supply and demand  more talent and fewer jobs means less  money per job furthermore if you're  taking stock options as compensation  your ability to get rich off those  options is far less likely in a bear  market taking equity as compensation is  far more risky for you than it is for  the founders offering you that equity so  just something to keep in mind now if  you're a software developer building a  product getting VC funding for that  product may be more difficult in the  future my personal philosophy is to  focus on building a profitable product  right from the beginning when you're not  dependent on external funding like a  bank or a VC you're much more likely to  survive economic swings now what if  you're just learning how to code should  you even bother well in my case I'm  passionate about building software I  enjoy the process of identifying  problems and then writing the solutions  and code to solve those problems even if  things continue falling it's still a  great skill to have  and will continue being the source of  many high-paying jobs in the future so  here's my interpretation of what's going  on we have this awesome tech industry  but maybe we've been a little  overzealous the coronavirus not really a  huge deal on its own is a catalyst  popping the bubble now when things start  to go down in a panic they go down a lot  faster than they did on their way up and  then economic realities start to set in  companies with poor cash flow will have  to go to extreme measures just to  survive now I just want to finish things  off by reminding you that I might be  completely wrong this whole thing might  just blow over in a week or two or maybe  Donald Trump in the Fed will step in and  save everybody I mostly just wanted to  provide a worst-case scenario type of  outlook in fact economic declines tend  to be a good thing long-term they create  a lot of opportunity for developers and  entrepreneurs and the painful parts tend  to be short-lived I'm gonna go ahead and  wrap things up there and tomorrow I will  be back with a regular code tutorial  thanks for watching like and subscribe  and I will see you in the next one
 

